\title{P11: where a warm pass of the final default configuration goes, and the three levers left for the executable}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\tag{platform}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Profiler "P11", 2026-11-24 (the windows: 04:24 to 05:11 UTC of 2026-11-25). The question: where does the time of the warm pass go now, in the final default configuration of the release (the machine as default engine with the switch set of wave 6 plus \code{\verb~|eager~}, \code{\verb~|skipargs~} and \code{\verb~|congruence~}; the executable a GraalVM Community Edition native image with the collection policy \code{\verb~|BySpaceAndTime~} and one scanner thread; the jar set frozen), and what are the three largest levers left for the single executable? This report repeats the itemisation of [rep-P10](rep-P10) on that configuration, on Mathlib, navier-stokes-euler and cslib, for the JVM launcher and for the executable, adds the wall-clock structure of the pass, the unit level against gruszka, and seven small experiments that change no code (batch load, two scanner threads, one worker, four image builds with other flags, three hand-off intervals of the pipelined load, five session caps, the largest units alone). Nothing in the repository was changed; no Oracle GraalVM was used.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\p{Warm pass, 8 workers, load pipelined with checking, the 8 measurement cores of the aarch64 development host with the other cores idle (section 1.2: that is not the condition of the benchmark tables), a run without options:}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Mathlib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{cslib}}
\tr{\td{the executable: pass s / CPU s}\td{14.46 / 109.4}\td{3.83 / 28.7}\td{5.22 / 38.9}}
\tr{\td{the JVM launcher: pass s / CPU s}\td{11.32 / 85.9}\td{3.01 / 22.7}\td{4.18 / 30.6}}
\tr{\td{gruszka, its arena run line with 8 workers, same windows: s / CPU s}\td{10.80 / 63.7}\td{2.65 / 15.6}\td{4.04 / 22.9}}
\tr{\td{the executable: workers / loader thread / environment thread, CPU s}\td{96.3 / 11.5 / 1.6}\td{24.5 / 2.5 / 1.7}\td{33.8 / 4.6 / 0.5}}
\tr{\td{the executable: the load alone on a core, s (the serial floor)}\td{11.13}\td{2.24}\td{4.28}}
\tr{\td{the executable / the JVM launcher}\td{1.28}\td{1.27}\td{1.25}}
\tr{\td{the executable / gruszka}\td{1.34}\td{1.45}\td{1.29}}
}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Where the time goes now.} The pass is bound by its total CPU on 8 cores: (workers + loader + environment thread) / 8 is 13.67 s of the 14.46 s on Mathlib in the image; the rest is 0.27 s of pauses and idle cores at both ends. Inside the workers the mixture is the one of rep-P10 without the fallback and without the proof comparison, at half the size: hash consing 25 \% (24.2 s of the image's 96.3 s on Mathlib, 3.0 s of wall clock), inference with universe levels 26 \% (levels alone 8.6 s), evaluation and reduction 19 \%, memo lookups 10 \%, conversion 8 \%, the term store 7 \%, around the machine 5 \% (of it the kernel 1.8 \%, the reference engine's fallback 0.7 \%), numeric computation 0.2 \%. The three corpora have the same shares to within 2 points, except more conversion and fewer levels on navier-stokes-euler (sections 2.1, 2.2).}
\li{\strong{The structure.} The loader is not the bottleneck today: the workers are parked for it for 0.8 to 3.5 \% of their time. But they are right behind it (the backlog is 1 to 2 \% of the file for the first two thirds of a Mathlib pass), and the loader thread alone needs 11.13 s on Mathlib and 4.28 s on cslib, 77 and 82 \% of the pass. Collections are 2 \% of the pass on both platforms. The reference engine decides 60, 16 and 7 units, one kind of decline, 1 \% or less of the time. Eight busy cores cost the same checking 1 to 6 \% more CPU than one worker alone (sections 3.1 to 3.5).}
\li{\strong{The image against the JVM.} The 3.1 s on Mathlib are code quality now: 2.4 s are the workers' user code (the image needs 25 \% more CPU for the same work, on all three corpora), 0.36 s the loader thread, 0.2 s page faults in the workers; the pauses are equal. The image is worst in the machine's own loops (evaluation proper 1.35 to 1.54 times, structural sameness 1.8, the pair table 1.4 to 1.6, the probes of closures 1.4 to 1.5) and equal where memory binds. No flag of the image build recovers any of it: twice and four times the inliner's budgets and \code{\verb~|-march=native~} are within 2 \% on the three corpora (sections 4.1 to 4.4).}
\li{\strong{Against gruszka.} RLVM does the reductions gruszka does (unfoldings within 3 to 9 \%) in 8 to 22 \% more ticks, and on the JVM its workers pay gruszka's price per tick on Mathlib and cslib (1.03 to 1.06 times; 1.24 on navier-stokes-euler). gruszka's lead over the executable is, in this order: the price of a tick in the image (1.33 to 1.57 times gruszka's), the load (5.4 s of CPU on 8 threads against 11.5 s on one, on Mathlib), and on navier-stokes-euler the large proof terms, where a tick of the machine gets dearer with the size of the session and gruszka's gets cheaper. The time is flat over the units: the 20 heaviest are 3 to 9 \% (sections 5.1 to 5.5).}
\li{\strong{The three levers} (section 6), by the size of what they attack in the executable: \strong{the code the image runs in the workers} (19.2 s of CPU, 2.4 s of 14.5 s on Mathlib; 16 to 17 \% of every pass; only a profile or C2 are known to recover it, and by hand perhaps a third: 0.8 s); \strong{the loader thread} (11.5 s of CPU, 1.4 s; directly 0.25 to 0.4 s, and it is the floor that lever 1 would hit on cslib after 14 \% and on Mathlib after 21 \%); \strong{the price of a tick in a large session} (ceiling 0.64 s of 3.9 s on navier-stokes-euler, 0.26 s on cslib, 0.76 s on Mathlib; expected half). Next in size, measured: the level stores (0.4 s per Mathlib pass for the copies at each publication alone). Together an eighth to a seventh of the pass, if all of it came true.}
\li{\strong{Not supported by the data} (section 7): tuning the collector, build flags, more scanner threads today, a faster hash table, a memo for universe instantiation, huge pages against TLB misses, the fallback, numeric computation, larger warm sessions, a list of slow units, and the reading that gruszka wins by skipping work.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was measured, and how}

\subtree{
\title{1.1 Binaries and configuration}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Distribution.} The shared distribution of the release, built on 2026-11-24 (03:18 UTC of 2026-11-25) by \src{scripts/build.sh} from the frozen jar set (jars sha256 \code{\verb~|162de4229b52~}): the executable \code{\verb~|rlvm~} (GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3 native image, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~}, \code{\verb~|-R:MaxHeapSize=13g~}, \code{\verb~|-R:InitialCollectionPolicy=BySpaceAndTime~}, the inline lists H10 of the machine jar) and the development launcher \code{\verb~|rlvm-jvm~} on the same jars (HotSpot of GraalVM CE 25 with C2: \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=8 -XX:+UseTransparentHugePages~}, heap of the machine \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}). Jars (sha256 prefix): rlvm-machine \code{\verb~|fa6d5d3bf377~}, rlvm-driver \code{\verb~|31fa08d9ce61~}, rlvm-load \code{\verb~|58b0056c0417~}, rlvm-store \code{\verb~|30979ee659b7~}, rlvm-export \code{\verb~|dc8c4f1ef79a~}, rlvm-level \code{\verb~|c57947b218af~}, rlvm-ref \code{\verb~|a20c24a26b7c~}, rlvm-admit \code{\verb~|398a117d4ad8~}, rlvm-inductive \code{\verb~|c15b14fffdd3~}, rlvm-nat \code{\verb~|b1dda7562adb~}, rlvm-api \code{\verb~|9abf2ef6620a~}, rlvm-cli \code{\verb~|cd4cbbee7ad1~}, rlvm-main \code{\verb~|3729609561e3~}, rlvm-truffle \code{\verb~|ad3256d0751d~}. No Oracle GraalVM was used for anything in this report.}
\li{\strong{Symbolised image.} A second CE image of exactly these jars with the flags that the build record of the distribution lists, plus \code{\verb~|-g~} (DWARF with inlined frames): the image the \code{\verb~|perf~} samples are taken from. In the arena container it makes the Mathlib pass in 14.44 to 14.79 s, the shipped image on the host in 14.44 to 14.46 s. No time in this report is from it unless said.}
\li{\strong{Engine.} A run without \code{\verb~|-X engine~}, which is the machine with its default switch set (\code{\verb~|rlvm --version~}: \code{\verb~|gated,pchk,expect,warm,facts,code,casetrees,probes,bodies,directrules,same,match,joint,lazycases,lazyeval,eager,skipargs,congruence~}), policy practical. Where the reference engine is meant it is named.}
\li{\strong{Run line.} \code{\verb~|rlvm[-jvm] --threads 8 --stats -X passes=N FILE~} and nothing else: every pass is a complete run (mode reload), the load is pipelined with the checking, one scanner step on the loader thread (\code{\verb~|scan-threads=1(auto)~}: with one scanner thread the scanning runs on the loader thread itself, so a pass has ten threads: eight workers, the loader thread, the environment thread). The JVM launcher got \code{\verb~|-XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions -XX:+DebugNonSafepoints -XX:FlightRecorderOptions:stackdepth=1024~} and its collection log in the profiled process, the image \code{\verb~|-XX:+PrintGC~}.}
\li{\strong{Corpora.} Mathlib (6.33 GB, 721,845 units), navier-stokes-euler (1.56 GB, 126,468 units), cslib (2.50 GB, 401,985 units), the exports of the benchmark tables.}
\li{\strong{gruszka.} The arena checker as built for the baseline tables (version \code{\verb~|ebc80dc~}; nothing was rebuilt), run in the arena container on the same 8 cores under \src{bench/measure.sh}: its arena run line with 8 workers (\code{\verb~|scripts/arena-run FILE 8~}), and the two steps of that line separately (\code{\verb~|susz pack --no-index~}, then \code{\verb~|gruszka FILE.susz 8~}); for the unit level its own switch \code{\verb~|SK_DECL_INSTR=1~}, which prints one line per declaration (index, name, nanoseconds, ticks, unfoldings).}
\li{\strong{Host.} aarch64, 10 Cortex-X925 and 10 Cortex-A725, 121 GB. Every number in seconds was taken inside a \src{bench/quiet.py} window on the 8 measurement cores (X925), one process per window at a time, heavy jobs behind \src{bench/slot.sh}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.2 The host was quiet, and that changes the comparison with gruszka}

\p{The windows of this report were taken between 04:24 and 05:11 UTC of 2026-11-25 (the night of 2026-11-24 local time), when hardly anything else was computing on the host: the load average was 0.2 when the first window started, and the only other load seen was this report's own image builds (the runs that overlapped with them were repeated). The benchmark tables of [rep-benchmarks](rep-benchmarks) were taken while the other 12 cores were busy; they share the memory bandwidth and the last-level cache with the measurement cores. The same binaries on the same cores, benchmark table against this report:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Mathlib, 8 workers, wall clock in s}\th{benchmark tables (other cores busy)}\th{this report (other cores idle)}}
\tr{\td{the executable, warm pass}\td{15.46}\td{14.46}}
\tr{\td{JVM launcher, warm pass}\td{12.44}\td{11.32 (passes 11.70, 11.32, 11.87)}}
\tr{\td{gruszka, its arena run line with 8 workers}\td{13.28 (second run 14.20)}\td{10.80}}
}

\p{So gruszka gains 19 \% from a quiet host and RLVM 6 to 9 \%: on a quiet host the executable is at 1.34 times gruszka on Mathlib, not at 1.09 to 1.23, and the JVM launcher at 1.05, not at 0.88. Everything below is from this report's windows, the three checkers in the same conditions. How much of the difference between the two nights is the other cores and how much is anything else that differed was not separated; what speaks for the cores is that gruszka's check needs 14 to 15 \% more CPU with eight workers than with one and RLVM's 1 to 6 \% (sections 3.4 and 5.3), and that a one-worker pass of the executable got 7 \% slower while image builds ran on the other cores. Which of the two conditions is the one that counts is not for this report to say; the arena's runner has 8 cores and nothing else running.}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.3 Method}

\p{The method, the classifiers and the item map are those of [rep-P10](rep-P10), sections 1.2 to 1.5, on the new binaries:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Seconds} are from unprofiled passes: wall clock from the pass line, CPU seconds per thread from the kernel's per-thread counters (on-CPU and run-queue nanoseconds, kernel time, page faults), polled every 100 ms (50 ms on the two smaller corpora) from a core outside the window's eight; collections from the collectors' logs. The timed pass is the fastest unprofiled later pass of the process (JVM: passes 2 to 4 on Mathlib, 4 to 6 on the smaller corpora; image: passes 2 to 4 or 2 to 5).}
\li{\strong{Shares} are from later passes of the same process on the JVM (\code{\verb~|jdk.CPUTimeSample~}, 2 ms of CPU per sample, attached with \code{\verb~|jcmd~} after the last unprofiled pass: passes 5 and 6 on Mathlib, 7 to 10 on the smaller corpora), and from a process of the symbolised image in the arena container (\code{\verb~|perf record~} with the fixed periods of rep-P10, attached after pass 2: \code{\verb~|cpu-clock~} 1 ms, cycles, instructions, cache refills of three levels, branch mispredictions, TLB walks, cycles stalled on memory).}
\li{An item's CPU seconds = its share of the worker samples x the workers' CPU seconds of the timed pass; its wall-clock seconds = CPU seconds / 8.}
\li{\strong{One item per method}: the item map of rep-P10 (591 rules, first match wins, innermost named frame; the reference-engine fallback and the decoding of a proof block are contexts that win over the innermost frame on the JVM). It already covered the methods of the switches \code{\verb~|eager~}, \code{\verb~|facts~}, \code{\verb~|probes~}, \code{\verb~|skipargs~} and \code{\verb~|congruence~}: no method of the final jars is without a rule (JVM: 0 of 80,089 Mathlib worker samples unassigned; image: 261 of 194,996, all of them JDK or runtime frames; the same on the two smaller corpora).}
\li{\strong{The wall-clock structure} is from the driver's own time account (\code{\verb~|-X stats-file~}, which also gives the series of loaded, announced and accepted units about every 100 ms) and from runs with \code{\verb~|-X load=batch~} (the file is loaded first and checked afterwards, so the load and the check each have the 8 cores).}
\li{\strong{The unit level} is the driver's unit trace (\code{\verb~|-X unit-trace~}: unit, worker, start, nanoseconds, ticks, outcome, size in nodes) of the same statistics runs, joined with the declaration names of the export and with gruszka's per-declaration lines. A unit's time is wall clock inside the unit, so it includes the run-queue waits and the pauses that fall into it: between two passes of one process about 13 \% of the time sits on different units. Per unit the smallest time over the traced passes is used (JVM: 2 passes on Mathlib, 4 on the smaller corpora; image: 3 and 4).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.4 Error bars}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Wall clock.} Later passes of one image process differ by 0.1 to 0.2 s on Mathlib (14.44 to 14.46 s in one process, 14.39 to 14.71 s over five processes) and by up to 0.12 s on the smaller corpora; JVM passes by up to 0.55 s on Mathlib (11.32 to 11.87 s; a second process 11.72 and 11.96 s). Worker CPU of later passes: within 1 \% in the image, within 2.5 \% on the JVM.}
\li{\strong{Sampling.} Worker samples: 80,089 (JVM) and 194,996 (image) on Mathlib, 39,577 and 100,616 on navier-stokes-euler, 55,703 and 137,335 on cslib: a 10 \% line has a standard error of 0.1 to 0.15 point. 68 CPU-time samples were lost on Mathlib (0.08 \%). 3.8 \% of the JVM samples are taken at the next safepoint instead of the interrupted pc.}
\li{\strong{The profiled pass is slower}: JFR +5.9 \% worker CPU and +8.4 \% wall on Mathlib (12.27 against 11.32 s), perf +0.5 to +1.2 \% and +1 to +1.7 \% (14.61 and 14.71 against 14.46 s).}
\li{\strong{Inlined frames.} As in rep-P10: the image's debug information attributes the one-line readers of the term store to their callers, so three lines trade places between the platforms (term store, conversion, inference); they are compared as a sum. A native sample has no caller: the store's share of the fallback and of proof-block decoding is split with the ratio measured on the JVM.}
\li{\strong{One host, one night, one architecture.} Nothing was measured on x86-64. A row is one process unless it says otherwise.}
}
}
}

