}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The pass is bound by its total CPU time on 8 cores.} Workers, loader thread and environment thread together need 13.67 s of 8 cores on Mathlib in the image and the pass takes 14.46 s; 10.69 against 11.32 s on the JVM; 3.58 against 3.83 s and 4.86 against 5.22 s in the image on the two smaller corpora. The remaining 5 to 9 \% are the pauses of the collections (0.27 s on Mathlib, all threads stopped) and cores that are idle at the start and at the end of the pass.}
\li{\strong{The workers are on a CPU for 82 to 85 \% of their life and runnable without one for 11 to 14 \%}: ten runnable threads on eight cores for as long as the file loads. They are parked for 0.8 to 4.2 \% in all (section 3.2).}
\li{\strong{The loader thread is a tenth of the CPU}: 11.46 s of 109.4 s on Mathlib in the image (10.5 \%), 2.47 of 28.7 s (8.6 \%) on navier-stokes-euler, 4.57 of 38.9 s (11.8 \%) on cslib; on the JVM 8.60, 1.91 and 3.76 s. It is one thread, so it is also the serial part of the pass (section 3.1).}
\li{\strong{Collections are 2 \% or less of every pass on both platforms}: 34 collections with 0.27 s of pauses per Mathlib pass in the image (rep-P10 on the image of that day: 121 collections, 2.18 s), 11 with 0.28 s on the JVM. The image still pays for memory in the kernel: 2.5 million page faults and 2.8 s of kernel time per Mathlib pass over all threads, against 96,000 and 0.3 s on the JVM (section 4.4).}
}
}

