}

\p{What the table says, against the itemisation of [rep-P10](rep-P10) on the default set of the afternoon (Mathlib, JVM launcher: 148.3 s of worker CPU in a pass of 21.93 s):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The workers need half the CPU}: 75.6 s instead of 148.3 s on the JVM, 96.3 s instead of 175.3 s in the image. What left is what the three switches were for: the fallback to the reference engine was 18.6 s and is 0.66 s; proof irrelevance and what it triggers was 40.8 s inclusive and is 1.2 s.}
\li{\strong{The shares did not move}: hash consing 25.9 \% (was 27.6 \%), memo lookups 9.5 \% (11.4 \%), evaluation and reduction 17.0 \% (16.4 \%), conversion 7.0 \% (4.4 \%), inference and levels 22.7 \% (15.5 \%), the term store 16.0 \% (11.4 \%), the fallback 0.9 \% (12.5 \%). The removed work was made of the same operations as the rest, so what remains is the same mixture, without the fallback.}
\li{\strong{Hash consing is still the largest item}: 19.6 s on the JVM and 24.2 s in the image on Mathlib, 2.4 and 3.0 s of wall clock. Nine tenths of it are probes and the writing of new records; table growth is 1.1 to 1.5 s.}
\li{\strong{Universe levels are the largest single line that is not a probe}: 7.45 s on the JVM, 8.60 s in the image on Mathlib (9.9 and 8.9 \% of the workers), 2.45 and 2.87 s on cslib (9.3 and 8.5 \%), 1.20 and 1.29 s on navier-stokes-euler (6.2 and 5.3 \%).}
\li{\strong{Reading the export's term nodes} is 7.87 s on the JVM on Mathlib (10.4 \%), where the readers' inlined frames are exact: \code{\verb~|Terms.arr~} 2.9 \%, \code{\verb~|Terms.spineLength~} 2.9 \%, \code{\verb~|Terms.kind~} 1.5 \% of the worker samples; a third of these reads are asked for by \code{\verb~|Infer.inferApp~}, an eighth by \code{\verb~|Same.terms~}.}
\li{\strong{Numeric computation is 0.1 to 0.2 \%} on all three corpora and both platforms.}
\li{\strong{The three corpora have the same profile.} navier-stokes-euler has more conversion (10.1 against 7.0 \% on the JVM) and fewer levels; cslib has Mathlib's shares to within 1.5 points on every group.}
}
}

