\tr{\td{- of it the reference engine (admission of inductive blocks) and the store work it asks for}\td{1.05}\td{1.00}\td{1.26}\td{1.34}\td{0.26}\td{0.29}}
}

\p{The JVM's samples have no kernel line (its 0.30 s of kernel time on Mathlib is inside the other lines). The environment thread decides 7,133 units on Mathlib, 1,618 on navier-stokes-euler and 4,377 on cslib, the inductive blocks and their kin, with the reference engine; navier-stokes-euler has as much of that work as Mathlib in a file a quarter of the size.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The wall-clock structure of the pass}

\subtree{
\title{3.1 Load alone, check alone, and the pass that does both}

\p{With \code{\verb~|-X load=batch~} the file is loaded first and checked afterwards: the loader thread has a core to itself, and then the workers have all eight. Later passes of one process, wall clock in s; the pipelined pass is the default:}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Mathlib: JVM}\th{image}\th{navier-stokes-euler: JVM}\th{image}\th{cslib: JVM}\th{image}}
\tr{\td{the load alone (one thread; its CPU is the same number)}\td{8.49}\td{11.13}\td{1.77}\td{2.24}\td{3.41}\td{4.28}}
\tr{\td{the check alone (8 workers on 8 cores)}\td{9.57}\td{12.05}\td{2.66}\td{3.32}\td{3.39}\td{4.25}}
\tr{\td{workers' CPU in the check alone, s}\td{74.0}\td{93.5}\td{19.2}\td{24.1}\td{26.0}\td{33.0}}
\tr{\td{batch pass = load + check}\td{18.08}\td{23.19}\td{4.45}\td{5.56}\td{6.81}\td{8.53}}
\tr{\td{\strong{the pipelined pass (default)}}\td{\strong{11.32}}\td{\strong{14.46}}\td{\strong{3.01}}\td{\strong{3.83}}\td{\strong{4.18}}\td{\strong{5.22}}}
\tr{\td{workers' CPU in the pipelined pass, s}\td{75.6}\td{96.3}\td{19.3}\td{24.5}\td{26.3}\td{33.8}}
\tr{\td{(workers + loader + environment thread) / 8}\td{10.69}\td{13.67}\td{2.82}\td{3.58}\td{3.80}\td{4.86}}
\tr{\td{the pipelined pass over the larger of load and check}\td{1.18}\td{1.20}\td{1.13}\td{1.15}\td{1.23}\td{1.22}}
\tr{\td{the load alone as a share of the pipelined pass}\td{75 \%}\td{77 \%}\td{59 \%}\td{58 \%}\td{82 \%}\td{82 \%}}
\tr{\td{the image with two scanner threads (the default before [log-RCAL](log-RCAL))}\td{-}\td{14.70}\td{-}\td{4.40}\td{-}\td{5.27}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Pipelining hides 8.7 s of a batch pass of 23.2 s on Mathlib in the image}, and the pass still takes 2.4 s longer than its check alone: the loader thread and the environment thread need 13 s of CPU from the same eight cores (1.6 s of wall clock), and the pass has 34 collections where the batch pass has 16.}
\li{\strong{The workers need 2 to 3 \% more CPU beside the loader than alone} (96.3 against 93.5 s in the image on Mathlib, 75.6 against 74.0 s on the JVM): what the pipelined pass adds is competition for cores, hardly any slower code.}
\li{\strong{A pass costs its CPU divided by 8, plus 6 to 10 \%}: 13.67 s of CPU per core on Mathlib in the image, 14.46 s of wall clock. Every CPU second saved anywhere, in a worker or in the loader, is worth an eighth of a second, and nothing else is worth anything while that holds.}
\li{\strong{It holds until the loader thread is the longest chain.} The load alone takes 11.13 s on Mathlib in the image, 77 \% of the pass. With L the loader's CPU and E the environment thread's, the workers stop being the bound when their CPU falls below about 8 times the load alone minus L and E: 76 s in the image on Mathlib (they are at 96.3 s, so after a gain of 21 \%), 58 s on the JVM (they are at 75.6 s). On cslib the limit is closer (29 s against 33.8 s in the image, a gain of 14 \%; 23 s against 26.3 s on the JVM), on navier-stokes-euler farther (14 s against 24.5 s in the image, but there the environment thread is the longer chain, section 3.3).}
\li{\strong{A second scanner thread does not pay today}, as [the decision on the settings of a plain run](dec-EGMG) found on cold runs: on Mathlib it takes the loader thread from 11.46 to 6.8 s of CPU and adds 7.3 s on the scanner threads, 2.7 s more in all, and the pass is 14.70 to 14.76 s instead of 14.46 s; on navier-stokes-euler 4.40 to 4.43 s instead of 3.83 to 3.95 s, with 22 collections instead of 8; on cslib it is even (5.27 to 5.32 s against 5.22 to 5.37 s). It would pay once the workers leave a core free, which is the same condition as above.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.2 How long the loader is the bottleneck}

\p{From the driver's time account of a statistics run (later pass, 8 workers; the run with statistics is 0.02 to 0.4 s slower than the timed one):}

\table{
\tr{\th{share of workers x wall clock of the check}\th{Mathlib: JVM}\th{image}\th{navier-stokes-euler: JVM}\th{image}\th{cslib: JVM}\th{image}}
\tr{\td{on a CPU}\td{84.3 \%}\td{85.0 \%}\td{81.2 \%}\td{81.2 \%}\td{82.0 \%}\td{82.6 \%}}
\tr{\td{runnable without a CPU}\td{11.9 \%}\td{11.5 \%}\td{13.8 \%}\td{13.3 \%}\td{14.5 \%}\td{11.3 \%}}
\tr{\td{parked: nothing announced, the loader has not delivered}\td{1.5 \%}\td{0.8 \%}\td{3.5 \%}\td{3.2 \%}\td{1.2 \%}\td{2.6 \%}}
\tr{\td{parked: nothing announced, the environment thread is behind}\td{0.1 \%}\td{0.0 \%}\td{0.6 \%}\td{0.3 \%}\td{0.6 \%}\td{0.6 \%}}
\tr{\td{parked in the tail (only busy claims left)}\td{0.0 \%}\td{0.0 \%}\td{0.1 \%}\td{0.2 \%}\td{0.1 \%}\td{0.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{blocked otherwise (collections, page faults)}\td{2.1 \%}\td{2.7 \%}\td{0.8 \%}\td{1.8 \%}\td{1.6 \%}\td{2.6 \%}}
\tr{\td{the load ends at, s of the pass}\td{10.29 of 11.68}\td{13.32 of 14.50}\td{1.96 of 3.03}\td{2.51 of 3.94}\td{3.60 of 4.24}\td{4.91 of 5.28}}
\tr{\td{the frontier is complete at, s}\td{10.29}\td{13.33}\td{2.32}\td{3.32}\td{3.60}\td{4.92}}
\tr{\td{last unit of the file announced to last unit checked, s}\td{1.38}\td{1.17}\td{0.70}\td{0.62}\td{0.64}\td{0.36}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The workers wait for the loader for 0.8 to 3.5 \% of their time}, 0.11 to 0.17 s per worker and pass on Mathlib. The loader is not the bottleneck of today's pass on any of the three corpora.}
\li{\strong{But the workers are right behind it.} On Mathlib in the image the units that are announced and not yet accepted are 0.1 to 2.1 \% of the file for the first 10 s of the pass (5,000 to 15,000 units; one publication is 16,384), 3.7 \% at 11 s, 9.8 \% at 13 s when the load is about to end, and the workers need 1.2 s more after the last unit is announced. The same on the JVM: 0.3 to 1.8 \% for 6 s, 12.6 \% at 10 s, 1.4 s of tail. On cslib in the image the backlog is 1.1 to 2.5 \% for 3 s and 7.2 \% at the end of the load, which is at 4.91 s of 5.28 s.}
\li{So for two thirds of a Mathlib pass the checking keeps pace with the file as it arrives, and only the heavy last third of Mathlib builds a queue. A pass that is a tenth faster in the workers would end with the load.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.3 The serial parts: what Amdahl leaves with 8 workers}

\table{
\tr{\th{serial part}\th{Mathlib: JVM}\th{image}\th{navier-stokes-euler: JVM}\th{image}\th{cslib: JVM}\th{image}}
\tr{\td{loader thread, alone on a core, s}\td{8.49}\td{11.13}\td{1.77}\td{2.24}\td{3.41}\td{4.28}}
\tr{\td{environment thread, CPU s (it follows the loader and gates the frontier)}\td{1.33}\td{1.59}\td{1.33}\td{1.74}\td{0.38}\td{0.48}}
\tr{\td{pauses of the collections, s}\td{0.28}\td{0.27}\td{0.05}\td{0.05}\td{0.09}\td{0.10}}
\tr{\td{the pass}\td{11.32}\td{14.46}\td{3.01}\td{3.83}\td{4.18}\td{5.22}}
\tr{\td{gruszka's whole run with 8 workers, same windows (section 5.1)}\td{10.80}\td{10.80}\td{2.65}\td{2.65}\td{4.04}\td{4.04}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The loader thread is the one long serial chain}: three quarters of the pass on Mathlib, four fifths on cslib. In the image its time alone is longer than gruszka's whole run on Mathlib (11.13 against 10.80 s) and 4.28 against 4.04 s on cslib: no engine, however fast, takes the executable to gruszka's wall clock on these two corpora while the load is one thread of that cost.}
\li{\strong{On navier-stokes-euler the chain is the environment thread.} It admits the inductive blocks with the reference engine, 1,618 units, 1.74 s of CPU in the image, in file order behind the loader; it is runnable without a core for 0.6 s of the pass, and the frontier is complete only at 3.32 s of 3.94 s (JVM: 2.32 of 3.03 s), 0.8 s after the load ended. The workers are parked for it for 0.3 to 0.6 \% of their time only; it would be the floor of a faster pass.}
\li{\strong{Collections are 0.27 s of a Mathlib pass on both platforms}; the collector is no longer a serial part that matters.}
\li{\strong{The tail is not idle time.} After the last unit is announced the eight workers are busy for 0.4 to 1.4 s; they are parked in the tail for 0.0 to 0.2 \% of their time.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.4 One worker}

\p{navier-stokes-euler and cslib with \code{\verb~|--threads 1~} (with one worker the driver loads and checks in turn, and the worker drives the environment pass itself, so the pass is the sum of its CPU):}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{navier-stokes-euler: JVM}\th{image}\th{cslib: JVM}\th{image}}
\tr{\td{pass with one worker, s}\td{22.17}\td{27.07}\td{29.82}\td{37.68}}
\tr{\td{CPU of the worker (checking and the environment pass), s}\td{20.2}\td{24.7 to 25.4}\td{26.3}\td{33.1}}
\tr{\td{the same work with 8 workers: workers + environment thread, CPU s}\td{20.63}\td{26.19}\td{26.67}\td{34.32}}
\tr{\td{8 workers against one, CPU}\td{1.02}\td{1.03 to 1.06}\td{1.01}\td{1.04}}
\tr{\td{wall clock, one worker / 8 workers}\td{7.4}\td{7.1}\td{7.1}\td{7.2}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Eight busy cores cost the same checking 1 to 6 \% more CPU} than one worker alone on the host: memory bandwidth and the shared cache, as [ins-RPLD](ins-RPLD) found (5 to 7 \% then). Nothing that the workers share shows up as CPU.}
\li{\strong{Eight workers are 7.1 to 7.4 times faster than one} in wall clock, pipelining included. The check phase alone scales by 7.4 to 7.8 (one worker's 24.7 s against 3.32 s of check alone on navier-stokes-euler in the image; 33.2 against 4.25 s on cslib).}
\li{Mathlib was not run with one worker.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.5 The reference engine's share}

\p{A plain run re-checks every rejection with the reference engine (none on these corpora) and gives it the units the machine declines. With the final set the machine declines one kind of unit only, with \code{\verb~|E_LIMIT~}: "K rule after the application of a let variable whose declared type is not the type of its value (finding E1-F6)". Each such unit is tried twice by the machine (120 declines for 60 units on Mathlib) and then decided by the reference engine.}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Mathlib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{cslib}}
\tr{\td{units the machine checks}\td{714,712}\td{124,850}\td{397,608}}
\tr{\td{of them declined and decided by the reference engine (\code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~} is not used: the fallback inside a plain run)}\td{60}\td{16}\td{7}}
\tr{\td{their time inside units, JVM / image, s}\td{0.74 / 1.11}\td{0.15 / 0.20}\td{0.04 / 0.05}}
\tr{\td{share of the time inside units, JVM / image}\td{0.99 / 1.22 \%}\td{0.83 / 0.85 \%}\td{0.15 / 0.16 \%}}
\tr{\td{steps of the reference engine in the pass (the fallback and the environment thread's admission of inductive blocks)}\td{5.28 M}\td{8.27 M}\td{0.82 M}}
\tr{\td{the fallback in the samples of the workers, JVM / image}\td{0.88 / 0.72 \%}\td{0.62 / 0.49 \%}\td{0.11 / 0.12 \%}}
}

\p{The units it spends it on, longest first (image, ms): on Mathlib \code{\verb~|AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isCoverDense_toOver_Spec~} 286, the private \code{\verb~|Tilde~} lemma of \code{\verb~|Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Modules.Tilde~} 147, \code{\verb~|AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isOneHypercoverDense_toOver_Spec~} 129, \code{\verb~|groupHomology.H1CoresCoinfOfTrivial_exact~} 42, \code{\verb~|LinearMap.exists_basis_basis_of_span_eq_top_of_mem_algebraMap~} 28, \code{\verb~|LinearMap.transvection.det~} 24; on navier-stokes-euler \code{\verb~|EulerSmoothTensorLimit.exists_smooth_limit~} 50, \code{\verb~|NavierStokes.UniformAngularReset.uniform_reset_branch~} 22, \code{\verb~|FormalMultilinearSeries.comp_assoc~} 21; on cslib \code{\verb~|FormalMultilinearSeries.comp_assoc~} 21, \code{\verb~|Cslib.Automata.NA.Buchi.buchiFamily_saturation~} 12. gruszka needs 1.3 to 5.9 ms for the first three of Mathlib (its profile with 4 workers of the baseline study). The environment thread uses the reference engine too, for the inductive blocks (section 2.5); that is admission, not fallback. The fallback is 1 \% or less of every corpus: it is not a lever any more.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The image against the JVM launcher}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 Where the seconds go}

\p{The same jars, the same pass, the timed passes of section 2.1: the executable needs 3.14 s more than the JVM launcher on Mathlib (14.46 against 11.32 s), 0.82 s more on navier-stokes-euler and 1.04 s more on cslib; 1.26 to 1.27 times the CPU on all three.}

\table{
\tr{\th{image - JVM}\th{Mathlib: CPU s}\th{wall s}\th{navier-stokes-euler: CPU s}\th{wall s}\th{cslib: CPU s}\th{wall s}}
\tr{\td{workers, user code: the image's code needs 25 to 27 \% more CPU than C2's}\td{+19.16}\td{+2.40}\td{+4.89}\td{+0.61}\td{+6.97}\td{+0.87}}
\tr{\td{workers, kernel: page faults (1.5 M against 361 per Mathlib pass)}\td{+1.58}\td{+0.20}\td{+0.26}\td{+0.03}\td{+0.58}\td{+0.07}}
\tr{\td{loader thread (of it kernel +0.85, +0.21, +0.34 s)}\td{+2.86}\td{+0.36}\td{+0.56}\td{+0.07}\td{+0.81}\td{+0.10}}
\tr{\td{environment thread}\td{+0.26}\td{+0.03}\td{+0.41}\td{+0.05}\td{+0.10}\td{+0.01}}
\tr{\td{JIT compiler threads and collector thread (the JVM's own)}\td{-0.44}\td{-0.06}\td{-0.19}\td{-0.02}\td{-0.15}\td{-0.02}}
\tr{\td{pauses of the collections (0.27 against 0.28 s on Mathlib)}\td{}\td{-0.01}\td{}\td{0.00}\td{}\td{+0.01}}
\tr{\td{cores idle otherwise (start and end of the pass)}\td{}\td{+0.22}\td{}\td{+0.08}\td{}\td{0.00}}
\tr{\td{\strong{sum}}\td{\strong{+23.42}}\td{\strong{+3.14}}\td{\strong{+5.93}}\td{\strong{+0.82}}\td{\strong{+8.31}}\td{\strong{+1.04}}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{It is code quality now, on every corpus}: three quarters or more of the gap are the workers' user code, a tenth is the loader thread, and memory management, which was half of the gap in [rep-P10](rep-P10) (pauses 1.4 s and page faults 0.9 s of 4.55 s), is 0.3 s of 3.14 s: the pauses are equal on the two platforms since the collection policy changed, and what is left is page faults.}
\li{\strong{The gap grew in relative terms as the work shrank}: C2's code needed 12 \% less CPU than the image's in rep-P10, and needs 20 \% less now (75.6 against 94.7 s of user code). The work that the three switches removed, the fallback's term substitution and the comparison of proofs, was the part where the two platforms were equal.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 Item by item: which items the image compiles worse}

\p{Ratio of the workers' CPU seconds, image / JVM, from the table of section 2.2:}

\table{
\tr{\th{item}\th{Mathlib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{cslib}\th{image - JVM on Mathlib, CPU s}}
\tr{\td{all worker CPU}\td{1.27}\td{1.27}\td{1.29}\td{+20.7}}
\tr{\td{1 hash consing}\td{1.24}\td{1.16}\td{1.24}\td{+4.6}}
\tr{\td{- probes: closures and tabling records}\td{1.41}\td{1.46}\td{1.54}\td{+2.1}}
\tr{\td{- probes: neutral applications}\td{1.36}\td{1.32}\td{1.35}\td{+1.4}}
\tr{\td{- probes: frames and spines}\td{1.03}\td{0.75}\td{0.84}\td{+0.1}}
\tr{\td{2 memo lookups}\td{1.33}\td{1.27}\td{1.34}\td{+2.4}}
\tr{\td{- the pair table}\td{1.61}\td{1.40}\td{1.60}\td{+0.9}}
\tr{\td{4 evaluation, code blocks, reduction}\td{1.41}\td{1.57}\td{1.43}\td{+5.3}}
\tr{\td{- evaluation proper}\td{1.35}\td{1.54}\td{1.39}\td{+3.2}}
\tr{\td{- working environment (\code{\verb~|Runs.push~}, \code{\verb~|enter~})}\td{4.7}\td{4.6}\td{4.8}\td{+1.25}}
\tr{\td{3 + 6 + 7 term store, conversion, inference and levels (compared as a sum, section 1.4)}\td{1.13}\td{1.15}\td{1.15}\td{+4.7}}
\tr{\td{- structural sameness (\code{\verb~|Same~})}\td{1.79}\td{1.79}\td{1.84}\td{+1.7}}
\tr{\td{- universe levels}\td{1.15}\td{1.08}\td{1.17}\td{+1.15}}
\tr{\td{- proof-block decoding}\td{0.89}\td{0.86}\td{0.85}\td{-0.4}}
\tr{\td{8 around the machine}\td{3.8}\td{3.9}\td{3.7}\td{+3.6}}
\tr{\td{- kernel}\td{image only}\td{}\td{}\td{+1.75}}
\tr{\td{- slab and stack growth}\td{4.0}\td{3.4}\td{3.4}\td{+0.8}}
\tr{\td{- the worklist itself}\td{6.3}\td{6.0}\td{0.8}\td{+0.4}}
\tr{\td{- array copy stubs, collector code, other runtime code}\td{image only}\td{}\td{}\td{+0.5}}
\tr{\td{- the reference-engine fallback}\td{1.05}\td{1.00}\td{1.3}\td{+0.0}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The three corpora give the same ratios}, to within 0.1 on every group: the gap is a property of the code, not of the corpus.}
\li{\strong{The image is worst where the work is the machine's own loops and data flow} (evaluation proper 1.35 to 1.54, structural sameness 1.8, the pair table 1.4 to 1.6, the probes of closures 1.4 to 1.5) \strong{and equal where the work is bound by memory} (proof-block decoding, the fallback, universe levels within 8 to 17 \%, the probes of frames).}
\li{\strong{Four lines exist almost only in the image}, 4.2 s of worker CPU on Mathlib together: the working environment (\code{\verb~|Runs.push~} and \code{\verb~|enter~} are separate code in the image and vanish into their callers under C2: 1.59 against 0.34 s), slab growth (1.11 against 0.28 s), the kernel (1.75 s) and the worklist's own steps (0.44 against 0.07 s). Three of the four are about fresh memory: a grown array in the image is new pages from the kernel, zeroed there and faulted in one by one; on the JVM it is a piece of a nursery that was touched long ago.}
\li{\strong{By class of the executing method} (the innermost inlined frame of a sample; CPU seconds of the workers on Mathlib, JVM against image): \code{\verb~|Values~} 13.9 and 19.2 (+5.3), \code{\verb~|Eval~} 10.8 and 14.9 (+4.1), \code{\verb~|Infer~} together with the readers of \code{\verb~|store.Terms~}, which the image attributes to their callers, 15.9 and 18.8 (+2.9), \code{\verb~|Same~} 1.9 and 3.7 (+1.8), \code{\verb~|Code~} 4.5 and 5.7 (+1.2), \code{\verb~|Machine~} 2.3 and 3.4 (+1.1), \code{\verb~|Pairs~} 1.2 and 2.0 (+0.8), \code{\verb~|Cargs~} 0.4 and 1.2 (+0.8), \code{\verb~|CodeStore~} 0.3 and 0.8, \code{\verb~|FactStore~} 0.2 and 0.6, the level binding of \code{\verb~|rlvm-api~} 0.1 and 0.5; equal within 0.2 s: \code{\verb~|Runs~}, \code{\verb~|Levels~}, \code{\verb~|IntListStore~}, \code{\verb~|Reduce~}; \code{\verb~|Conv~} +0.4. This is the work list of a pass over the machine for the image's sake (section 6.1).}
\li{\strong{By worker count} the two platforms scale alike on navier-stokes-euler: the checking and the environment pass need 24.7 to 25.4 s of CPU with one worker in the image and 26.2 s with eight, 20.2 and 20.6 s on the JVM (section 3.4).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 What does not recover it: flags of the image build}

\p{Four more images of the same jars, each the shipped flags plus one change, interleaved with the shipped executable inside one window per corpus (median later pass of a process of 3 or 4 passes; navier-stokes-euler two rounds; wall clock in s, in brackets the workers' CPU against the shipped executable):}

\table{
\tr{\th{image}\th{Mathlib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{cslib}}
\tr{\td{the shipped executable}\td{14.54}\td{3.88}\td{5.28}}
\tr{\td{the same flags built again}\td{not run}\td{3.89 (+0.2 \%)}\td{not run}}
\tr{\td{inliner budgets doubled (\code{\verb~|-H:BaseTargetSpending=240 -H:InliningCoefficient=0.04~})}\td{14.59 (+0.3 \%)}\td{3.96 (-0.4 \%)}\td{5.32 (+0.4 \%)}}
\tr{\td{inliner budgets times four, with \code{\verb~|-H:InlinedCompilerNodeLimit=40000 -H:CallGraphSizeLimit=2400~}}\td{14.48 (0.0 \%)}\td{3.92 (-1.6 \%)}\td{5.32 (+0.2 \%)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-march=native~}}\td{14.56 (+0.4 \%)}\td{3.92 (-0.8 \%)}\td{5.29 (+0.8 \%)}}
}

\p{No flag moves the pass or the workers' CPU by more than 2 \%, which is the spread between rows. The images with larger budgets are 0.5 and 0.9 MB larger, so the inliner did inline more; it bought nothing. What the image lacks is not inlining volume and not instruction-set extensions. Three things are known to be worth something: the inline lists of the machine that are already in the build (0.7 to 4.4 \% of the machine's CPU, [the switch reference](design-switches-document)); \code{\verb~|-O3~} over \code{\verb~|-O2~}, already in the build; and a profile, which only Oracle GraalVM can use: the optional profile-guided executable runs the Mathlib pass in 0.84 times the default executable's time in the benchmark tables ([rep-benchmarks](rep-benchmarks); not re-measured here, no Oracle GraalVM was used). The JVM launcher is at 0.78.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.4 Memory in the image: collections are small, fresh pages are not free}

\table{
\tr{\th{per Mathlib pass}\th{JVM launcher}\th{image}}
\tr{\td{collections}\td{9 young + 2 complete}\td{34 young}}
\tr{\td{pauses, s}\td{0.28}\td{0.27}}
\tr{\td{memory freed by the collections, GB}\td{4.9}\td{2.3}}
\tr{\td{minor page faults, all threads}\td{96,000}\td{2,490,000}}
\tr{\td{kernel time, all threads, s}\td{0.32}\td{2.83}}
\tr{\td{peak resident set of the process, MiB}\td{6,404}\td{7,548}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The collector is settled}: 0.27 s of pauses, 0.05 s of collector code on the workers. \code{\verb~|BySpaceAndTime~} did what [log-RCAL](log-RCAL) says.}
\li{\strong{Fresh pages cost 2.8 s of CPU per pass}, 0.35 s of wall clock: 1.5 million faults in the workers (5.9 GB of new pages per pass), 0.86 million in the loader thread. The JVM allocates the same 5 GB per pass out of a nursery of 1 GB that it touched once, on huge pages.}
\li{\strong{About three fifths of the workers' faults are the copies of the level tables.} Each publication of the pipelined load makes every worker copy the level tables at its next reset ([ins-E1DU](ins-E1DU)). The image with other hand-off intervals, later passes of one process each, Mathlib:}
}

\table{
\tr{\th{publications per pass}\th{pass, s}\th{process CPU, s}\th{workers' CPU, s}\th{workers' kernel time, s}\th{workers' page faults}\th{collections}\th{pauses, s}}
\tr{\td{205 (\code{\verb~|-X publish-scale=1~})}\td{15.04 to 15.17}\td{113.5}\td{99.3 to 99.9}\td{2.4}\td{2.5 M}\td{59}\td{0.54}}
\tr{\td{52 (default)}\td{14.59 to 14.61}\td{110.5}\td{96.8 to 97.3}\td{1.5}\td{1.5 M}\td{34}\td{0.27}}
\tr{\td{8 (\code{\verb~|-X publish-scale=1 -X publish-units=131072 -X publish-mib=1024~})}\td{15.27 to 15.28}\td{108.5}\td{95.0 to 95.9}\td{0.85}\td{0.75 M}\td{19}\td{0.14}}
}

\p{From 205 to 52 publications the workers save 2.8 s of CPU, 1.0 million faults and 0.27 s of pauses, and the pass 0.5 s; from 52 to 8 another 1.6 s, 0.77 million faults and 0.13 s of pauses, but the pass gets 0.7 s longer, because eight pieces feed the workers too coarsely. So at the default a publication costs the workers about 36 ms of CPU, 17,000 page faults (68 MiB of new pages) and 3 ms of pause, 52 times per pass: 1.9 s of worker CPU, 0.9 million faults and 0.15 s of pauses, about 0.4 s of the 14.5 s. A level store that the workers share instead of copying would give that back and would let the file be published in small pieces again.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What was not separated:} who faults the other 0.6 million pages of the workers. A native sample has no caller, so a page fault cannot be attributed to an allocation site; the candidates are the slab growths of the sessions that outgrow the kept slabs (958 MB grown in 203 growths per pass, by the machine's own count) and the eden of the collector, which is fresh memory after every collection.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Against gruszka}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 The same windows}

\p{gruszka in the arena container on the same 8 cores, one run per cell (the check alone was run twice and repeats to 0.04 s), against RLVM's timed passes of section 2.1. How it works is in [the study of gruszka and pestka](study-gruszka-pestka): the export is first packed into a binary columnar file on all threads, the shared part of that file is loaded, and the proof-private records stay bytes until a worker checks their theorem.}

\table{
\tr{\th{8 workers, 8 cores}\th{Mathlib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{cslib}}
\tr{\td{gruszka, its arena run line: wall s / CPU s}\td{10.80 / 63.7}\td{2.65 / 15.6}\td{4.04 / 22.9}}
\tr{\td{- packing the export (\code{\verb~|susz pack --no-index~}): wall s / CPU s}\td{1.80 / 5.4}\td{0.36 / 1.2}\td{0.73 / 2.2}}
\tr{\td{- the check of the packed file: wall s / CPU s}\td{8.80 / 58.4}\td{2.24 / 14.4}\td{3.27 / 20.9}}
\tr{\td{- peak resident set of the check, MiB}\td{2,876}\td{967}\td{1,503}}
\tr{\td{- user-space instructions of the run line}\td{421 G}\td{120 G}\td{151 G}}
\tr{\td{the executable, warm pass: wall s / CPU s}\td{14.46 / 109.4}\td{3.83 / 28.7}\td{5.22 / 38.9}}
\tr{\td{the JVM launcher, warm pass: wall s / CPU s}\td{11.32 / 85.9}\td{3.01 / 22.7}\td{4.18 / 30.6}}
\tr{\td{the executable / gruszka: wall, CPU}\td{1.34, 1.72}\td{1.45, 1.84}\td{1.29, 1.70}}
\tr{\td{the JVM launcher / gruszka: wall, CPU}\td{1.05, 1.35}\td{1.14, 1.46}\td{1.03, 1.34}}
}

\p{RLVM needs 1.7 to 1.8 times gruszka's CPU in the image and stays within 1.3 to 1.45 of its wall clock, because RLVM checks while it loads and gruszka packs and loads before it checks: its packing is 0.36 to 1.80 s of wall clock in which nothing is checked.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.2 The same work, at a higher price per step}

\p{Both checkers count the iterations of their loops ("ticks"; the two counters are not the same instrument) and their unfoldings. Whole corpus, 8 workers:}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Mathlib}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{cslib}}
\tr{\td{ticks: RLVM's machine / gruszka}\td{575.8 M / 471.2 M = 1.22}\td{145.3 M / 134.5 M = 1.08}\td{216.7 M / 177.8 M = 1.22}}
\tr{\td{unfoldings: RLVM (delta calls that are not memo hits) / gruszka}\td{17.9 M / 19.2 M}\td{3.8 M / 3.9 M}\td{7.3 M / 6.7 M}}
\tr{\td{CPU per tick, gruszka (its check, load included), ns}\td{124}\td{107}\td{117}}
\tr{\td{CPU per tick, RLVM's workers on the JVM, ns}\td{131 (1.06 x)}\td{133 (1.24 x)}\td{121 (1.03 x)}}
\tr{\td{CPU per tick, RLVM's workers in the image, ns}\td{167 (1.35 x)}\td{168 (1.57 x)}\td{156 (1.33 x)}}
\tr{\td{records made per tick by the machine}\td{0.50}\td{0.48}\td{0.52}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{RLVM does the reductions gruszka does}: the unfoldings agree to 3 to 9 \%.}
\li{\strong{On the JVM the workers pay gruszka's price per tick on Mathlib and cslib} (1.03 to 1.06 times) and 1.24 times on navier-stokes-euler; \strong{in the image 1.33 to 1.57 times.} For the executable the price per step is the image's code (section 4), not the design of the machine.}
\li{\strong{RLVM takes 8 to 22 \% more ticks.} Unit by unit (below) the two counters agree to within a quarter on the units that hold half of the ticks; RLVM takes more than 1.25 times gruszka's ticks on a third of the units of Mathlib (239,000 units, +124 M ticks) and fewer than 0.8 times on 208,000 units (-20 M). The excess is not in one kind of declaration: ordinary theorems 1.22 times, auxiliary proofs 1.28, definitions 1.21, instances 1.21, equation lemmas 1.84 (9 M ticks of 575 M), matchers 1.02; and not in one size: 1.31 times below 100 nodes, 1.16 to 1.26 above.}
\li{\strong{The rest of the CPU is the loader}: 11.46 s on Mathlib in the image against 5.4 s of packing plus gruszka's load of the packed file (inside its 58.4 s; its documents give 1.24 s of wall clock for Mathlib).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.3 The unit level}

\p{Time inside units, smallest time of each unit over the traced passes with 8 workers (section 1.3); gruszka per declaration from its own switch with one worker (with 8 workers the switch distorts the run: the checker writes every line to an unbuffered standard error under a lock, and the cslib check takes 10.1 s instead of 3.3 s), so gruszka's column is free of what eight busy cores cost and RLVM's is not. That is 14 to 15 \% for gruszka (its check needs 12.7 s of CPU with one worker and 14.4 s with eight on navier-stokes-euler, 18.1 and 20.8 s on cslib) and 1 to 6 \% for RLVM (section 3.4); at equal worker counts the ratios of this section are lower by that much.}

