\p{What the unit level says:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The time is flat.} The 20 heaviest units are 9.1 \% of the time inside units on navier-stokes-euler, 5.2 \% on cslib and 3.3 \% on Mathlib; the 1,000 heaviest 27 \%, 15 \% and 11.5 \%. There is no list of units whose repair moves a pass.}
\li{\strong{navier-stokes-euler is large proof terms.} Four proofs of 270,000 to 650,000 nodes (\code{\verb~|quadraticBilin._proof_6~} and \code{\verb~|_proof_9~} of two modules) take 0.19 to 0.74 s each in the image, 1.7 s together (7.3 \%), at 4 to 5 ticks per node: plain inference of a very large term, almost no unfolding (gruszka: 1,200 to 8,600 unfoldings in 1.2 to 3.1 M ticks). The same number of ticks in both checkers. The four take the executable 1.69 s and gruszka 0.57 s with one worker: 3.0 times, where the units below 1,000 nodes are at 1.5 to 1.8 times. Two of them were profiled alone (the block of 1,024 consecutive units that holds them, \code{\verb~|-X shard=48/124~}, in which they are 90 \% of the time; two workers, JVM launcher under JFR and the symbolised image under \code{\verb~|perf~}): hash consing is 41 \% of the JVM's worker time there against 27 \% in the corpus (the probes of closures and tabling records 14.4 against 7.3 \%, of neutral applications 10.8 against 5.0 \%) and the pair table 8.4 against 3.3 \%; in the image hash consing is 35 against 25 \%, the pair table 7.2 against 3.6 \%, the kernel 10.4 against 1.4 \%, and 26 \% of the cycles are stalled on memory against 13 \%. A proof of 650,000 nodes is one session of 3.4 M ticks: its tables are far out of the cache, and in the image every doubling of a slab is fresh pages.}
\li{\strong{cslib has four units of another kind}: the proofs \code{\verb~|Char.of_any_eq_false_aux._proof_*~}, \code{\verb~|Char.any._proof_1~} and \code{\verb~|Char.all._proof_1~} of \code{\verb~|Batteries.Data.Char.Basic~} (they are in Mathlib too). They enter eager reduction, the switch \code{\verb~|eager~} keeps them on the machine, and the machine takes 1.0 to 3.2 M ticks and 0.13 to 0.53 s for each in the image where gruszka takes 1,500 to 2,800 ticks and 0.2 to 0.4 ms. Together 1.3 s of the image's 31.5 s inside units on cslib (4.2 \%; JVM 0.68 s) and 1.3 s of 90.6 s on Mathlib. Which rule lets gruszka decide them without the computation was not determined; the machine follows the official strategy there ([eager reduction on the machine](design-eager-reduction)).}
\li{\strong{The units the reference engine decides} are 8 to 165 times gruszka's time each and 0.2 to 1.2 \% of the time together (section 3.5).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.4 Which of gruszka's mechanisms account for its lead}

\p{From the study, checked against these numbers:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Not "what not to compute".} The lists of skipped work are now nearly the same on the two sides: unfoldings within 3 to 9 \%, ticks within 8 to 22 \%. The two shortcuts for proof arguments and unread arguments, the direct case trees and the lazy evaluation of the default set are RLVM's versions of gruszka's list. What gruszka has and RLVM has in a narrower form is the reuse of checked facts across declarations: gruszka keeps, per shared term, that it passed check mode under a list of level parameters, and the lambda bodies and codomains of checked definitions; RLVM's \code{\verb~|pchk~} keeps it for closed terms without level parameters only. Whether that is where the 8 to 22 \% of ticks are could not be told from the unit level: the excess is spread over every kind and size of declaration.}
\li{\strong{The price of a step, for the executable}: values, their memo words and their tables in flat arrays compiled by an optimising ahead-of-time compiler with a profile (gruszka's build is profile-guided, with link-time optimisation); RLVM's machine has the same shape of data, and C2 compiles its steps to gruszka's price on two of the three corpora. The image does not.}
\li{\strong{The load.} gruszka packs on 8 threads (5.4 s of CPU, 1.8 s of wall clock on Mathlib) and materialises a third of the expressions; RLVM's loader thread also leaves the proof-private records packed, and still needs 11.5 s on one thread in the image. On cslib and Mathlib the executable's load alone is longer than gruszka's whole run (section 3.3).}
\li{\strong{Large units.} On navier-stokes-euler gruszka's tick gets cheaper as the unit grows (112 ns below 100 nodes, 67 to 71 ns above 10,000 nodes, one worker) and RLVM's gets dearer (151 to 208 ns in the image; on \code{\verb~|quadraticBilin._proof_6~} 221 ns in the image and 155 ns on the JVM against 168 and 133 ns over the corpus). What makes RLVM dearer there is the size of the session, not the kind of work (sections 5.5 and 6.3).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.5 Longer sessions: fewer ticks, no gain}

\p{gruszka's warm sessions hold 8 MiB; the machine's cap is 1 MiB, set by the processor cache ([ins-FJ5R](ins-FJ5R)). If the extra ticks were work that a longer memory across declarations saves, larger sessions would show it. The image with other caps (\code{\verb~|-X machine.session-kib~}, with the driver's \code{\verb~|-X session-mib~} raised accordingly), later passes of one process:}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{session cap}\th{later passes, s}\th{workers' CPU, s}\th{ticks}\th{worker CPU per tick, ns}\th{records}\th{session resets}\th{collections, pauses s}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler (gruszka: 134.5 M ticks)}\td{256 KiB}\td{4.11 4.08}\td{26.0}\td{174.1 M}\td{149}\td{93.8 M}\td{13,664}\td{8, 0.05}}
\tr{\td{}\td{1 MiB (default)}\td{3.93 3.87}\td{25.1}\td{145.2 M}\td{173}\td{70.2 M}\td{3,538}\td{8, 0.05}}
\tr{\td{}\td{4 MiB}\td{3.92 3.89}\td{25.4}\td{126.0 M}\td{202}\td{56.2 M}\td{959}\td{8, 0.04}}
\tr{\td{}\td{16 MiB}\td{4.28 4.18}\td{26.8}\td{115.9 M}\td{231}\td{49.7 M}\td{424}\td{17, 0.14}}
\tr{\td{}\td{64 MiB}\td{4.36 4.31}\td{27.6}\td{113.5 M}\td{243}\td{48.3 M}\td{384}\td{20, 0.11}}
\tr{\td{cslib (gruszka: 177.8 M ticks)}\td{256 KiB}\td{5.28 5.18}\td{32.7}\td{230.3 M}\td{142}\td{124.4 M}\td{20,548}\td{12, 0.09}}
\tr{\td{}\td{1 MiB (default)}\td{5.35 5.36}\td{33.9}\td{216.7 M}\td{157}\td{113.5 M}\td{6,051}\td{13, 0.11}}
\tr{\td{}\td{4 MiB}\td{5.58 5.69}\td{36.8}\td{208.2 M}\td{177}\td{105.7 M}\td{2,279}\td{12, 0.09}}
\tr{\td{}\td{16 MiB}\td{5.86 5.89}\td{38.3}\td{203.7 M}\td{188}\td{101.8 M}\td{1,585}\td{15, 0.13}}
\tr{\td{}\td{64 MiB}\td{5.82 5.87}\td{38.5}\td{203.6 M}\td{189}\td{101.6 M}\td{1,649}\td{14, 0.12}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib (gruszka: 471.2 M ticks)}\td{1 MiB (default)}\td{14.48 14.52}\td{97.1}\td{575.6 M}\td{169}\td{289.5 M}\td{14,964}\td{34, 0.27}}
\tr{\td{}\td{4 MiB}\td{15.46 15.40}\td{105.5}\td{550.8 M}\td{192}\td{267.5 M}\td{5,233}\td{30, 0.23}}
\tr{\td{}\td{16 MiB}\td{16.58 16.66}\td{112.3}\td{539.6 M}\td{208}\td{257.1 M}\td{3,319}\td{50, 0.47}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{On navier-stokes-euler a longer memory saves ticks}: 13 \% at 4 MiB, 20 \% at 16 MiB, 22 \% at 64 MiB, down to fewer ticks than gruszka takes. The extra ticks of the default configuration on this corpus are work that is done again after a reset. \strong{On cslib and Mathlib it saves 4 to 6 \%}, and at 16 MiB RLVM still takes 15 \% more ticks than gruszka: there the excess is not the length of the session.}
\li{\strong{The pass gets shorter nowhere, because the tick gets dearer faster}: 173, 202 and 231 ns of worker CPU per tick at 1, 4 and 16 MiB on navier-stokes-euler, 157, 177 and 188 ns on cslib, 169, 192 and 208 ns on Mathlib, where a cap of 4 MiB costs 0.9 s of the pass and one of 16 MiB 2.1 s.}
\li{\strong{Where the dearer tick is.} navier-stokes-euler at 4 MiB under \code{\verb~|perf~} against the default cap, CPU per tick of the workers: hash consing 42 to 60 ns (the probes of closures 14.1 to 20.1, of neutral applications 8.8 to 12.7, growth and rehash of the value table 2.5 to 6.9), the pair table 6.1 to 9.6 ns, everything else together 120 to 134 ns; 3.93 instructions per cycle instead of 4.30, and 19 \% of the cycles stalled on memory instead of 13 \%. It is the tables that find a value again, and nothing else.}
\li{\strong{A smaller cap is not a general gain either}: 256 KiB is 1 to 3 \% faster than the default on cslib (142 ns per tick) and 5 \% slower on navier-stokes-euler.}
\li{Mathlib was run at 1, 4 and 16 MiB only. Every row is one process.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. The three largest levers for the single executable}

\p{The rule of section 3.1 prices every lever: while the pass is bound by its CPU, a CPU second saved in any thread is worth an eighth of a second, 6 to 10 \% of idle and pauses are on top, and the loader thread alone is the floor (11.1 s on Mathlib, 2.2 s on navier-stokes-euler, 4.3 s on cslib in the image). The levers are ordered by the measured size of the item they attack in the executable. None of them was implemented or prototyped here; each estimate says what it rests on. Each lever also has a short insight: [the image's code](ins-TVGI), [the loader thread](ins-DBTJ), [the tick in a large session](ins-0IR8).}

\subtree{
\title{6.1 Lever 1: the code the image runs in the workers}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What it is.} The same jars need 25 to 27 \% more user CPU in the workers when GraalVM CE compiles them ahead of time than when C2 compiles them with a profile: the probes of closures 1.4 to 1.5 times, evaluation proper 1.35 to 1.54, structural sameness 1.8, the pair table 1.4 to 1.6, and four lines that C2 all but removes (working environment, slab growth, the worklist's steps, the kernel).}
\li{\strong{Measured size.} 19.2 s of worker CPU on Mathlib, 4.9 s on navier-stokes-euler, 7.0 s on cslib: 2.4, 0.61 and 0.87 s of the pass, 16 to 17 \% of it on every corpus, and three quarters of the executable's distance to the JVM launcher. The loader thread adds 2.9, 0.6 and 0.8 s of CPU of the same kind.}
\li{\strong{Estimate, and what it rests on.} There are three known ways to better code and this report closes the cheapest. (a) Build flags: measured, nothing (section 4.3). (b) A profile: 0.84 times the pass on Mathlib in the benchmark tables, 2.3 s of 14.5 s, but only Oracle GraalVM has it, so by the standing policy it stays the optional build. (c) Changes in the machine's source that give the ahead-of-time compiler by hand what a profile gives C2. The four image-only lines are 4.2 s of worker CPU on Mathlib (0.5 s of wall clock) and have a visible cause each: \code{\verb~|Runs.push~} and \code{\verb~|enter~} kept as calls, growth paths that are reached often enough to show, fresh pages. If a pass over them and over the three loops with the worst ratio takes a third of the gap, that is 6 s of CPU and 0.8 s on Mathlib (5 \%), 0.2 s on navier-stokes-euler, 0.3 s on cslib. The third is a guess between two measurements: the inline lists already in the build were worth 0.7 to 4.4 \% of the machine's CPU, a full profile is worth 16 \%.}
\li{\strong{What would have to change.} \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~}: \code{\verb~|Eval~} (package M2), \code{\verb~|Values~} and \code{\verb~|Runs~} (M1), \code{\verb~|Machine~} and the slab growth (M6), \code{\verb~|Same~} and \code{\verb~|Pairs~} (M4), with the class list of section 4.2 as the order of work; and the inline lists of the build (\code{\verb~|rlvm-machine/h10~}, \src{scripts/build.sh}), which the owner of the facade generates. A different answer to the same item is a decision about packaging, not an engineering task: the JVM launcher is 3.1 s faster on Mathlib warm today and loses cold on small inputs (0.74 against 3.03 s on Init in the benchmark tables).}
\li{\strong{Risk for the verdict.} Cost only, if the changes are restructurings: no rule changes. But they are in the hottest code of the machine, where a slip is a wrong value. The machine's gate that compares tick counts declaration by declaration is the right net: a restructuring must leave every count identical.}
\li{\strong{Gate.} The fast group and the full gate of the machine with identical tick counts, the hostile matrix, the gates of the default configuration through the executable; then the guard of [the rule for a default](dec-YP0N), measured in the image on all eight corpora, because this lever exists only there and a gain on the JVM launcher would say nothing.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.2 Lever 2: the loader thread}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What it is.} One thread scans the file, links, loads and builds the store. It is a tenth of the pass's CPU, and it is the longest serial chain of the pass.}
\li{\strong{Measured size.} 11.46 s of CPU on Mathlib in the image (10.5 \% of the pass's CPU, 1.43 s of wall clock), 2.47 s on navier-stokes-euler (8.6 \%), 4.57 s on cslib (11.8 \%). Alone on a core it takes 11.13, 2.24 and 4.28 s: 77, 58 and 82 \% of today's pass. gruszka's whole run is 10.80 s on Mathlib and 4.04 s on cslib. In the image the thread needs 1.33 times the JVM's CPU on Mathlib (8.60 s); 1.59 s of the difference is kernel time for 0.86 million page faults, the rest is the same code compiled worse (section 2.5).}
\li{\strong{Estimate.} Direct, at an eighth of a second per CPU second: the page faults and the array copies of the growing store are 1.9 s on Mathlib (store arrays sized once from the size of the file, instead of grown as it arrives, would take the copies and the faults of the abandoned arrays: perhaps half, 1 s); the scanner and the linker at gruszka's cost of reading (5.4 s of packing plus its load, against 9.3 s of scanning, linking, loading and packing here) another 1 to 2 s. Together 2 to 3 s of CPU: 0.25 to 0.4 s on Mathlib (2 to 3 \%), 0.1 s on cslib. Indirect, and larger: it is the floor. On cslib the workers are 14 \% above the point where the loader binds, on Mathlib 21 \%; lever 1 alone at full size would end at the loader or just behind it on both (a pass of about 4.5 s instead of 4.2 s on cslib). Splitting the thread is measured: two scanner threads take the loader thread from 11.46 to 6.8 s on Mathlib for 2.7 s more CPU in all, a loss today and the right setting once the workers are a fifth faster.}
\li{\strong{What would have to change.} \code{\verb~|rlvm-export~} (scanner), \code{\verb~|rlvm-load~} (linker, loader, the rule for scanner threads in \code{\verb~|rlvm-main~}), \code{\verb~|rlvm-store~} (allocation of the store's arrays): the owner of the loader. The scanner-thread rule is a default of the executable and is decided by [the settings of a plain run](dec-EGMG); it would have to be measured again after any gain in the workers.}
\li{\strong{Risk for the verdict.} The loader decides what both engines see. Its invariant is that the store is word for word the store of the record-by-record path; allocation and thread layout do not touch it, a change in how records are scanned or packed does. A load-level decline must stay per unit.}
\li{\strong{Gate.} The loader's store-equality tests on every corpus, the gates of the default configuration (the mutant corpora and the hostile exports exercise the reader's failure paths), the invariance of the verdict under worker count and interleaving, and the guard on eight corpora in the image.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.3 Lever 3: the price of a tick in a large session}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What it is.} A tick of the machine gets dearer as its session grows: the value table, the tabling records and the pair table leave the cache. The session cap of 1 MiB exists because of it ([ins-FJ5R](ins-FJ5R)), and a unit that is larger than the cap cannot be helped by the cap. The cap in turn throws away what a longer memory would save.}
\li{\strong{Measured size.} On navier-stokes-euler in the image a tick costs 173 ns of worker CPU at the default cap, 202 ns at 4 MiB and 231 ns at 16 MiB, while the ticks fall from 145.2 M to 126.0 M and 115.9 M, below gruszka's 134.5 M (section 5.5): the pass gets no shorter (3.87 to 3.93 s, 3.89 to 3.92 s, 4.18 to 4.28 s). On cslib 157, 177 and 188 ns for 216.7, 208.2 and 203.7 M ticks. On Mathlib 169, 192 and 208 ns for 575.6, 550.8 and 539.6 M ticks, and the pass goes from 14.5 to 15.4 and 16.6 s. At 4 MiB on navier-stokes-euler the whole increase is in the tables that find a value again: hash consing goes from 42 to 60 ns per tick (probes of closures and neutral applications, growth and rehash of the value table), the pair table from 6.1 to 9.6 ns, everything else from 120 to 134 ns. In the two largest units of navier-stokes-euler, profiled alone (section 5.3), hash consing is 41 \% of the JVM's time and 35 \% of the image's against 27 and 25 \% in the corpus, the pair table 8.4 and 7.2 \% against 3.3 and 3.6 \%, the kernel 10.4 \% of the image's against 1.4 \%, and 26 \% of the image's cycles are stalled on memory against 13 \%. Units of 1,000 nodes and more are half of the executable's time inside units on that corpus (11.6 of 23.0 s) and are where its ratio to gruszka is worst (1.9 to 3.0 times against 1.5 to 1.8 below).}
\li{\strong{Estimate.} The ceiling is a machine whose tick costs the same at 16 MiB as at 1 MiB: navier-stokes-euler would need 115.9 M ticks at 173 ns, 20.0 s of worker CPU instead of 25.1 s, 0.64 s of the 3.9 s pass (16 \%); cslib 31.8 instead of 33.9 s, 0.26 s (5 \%). Mathlib 539.6 M ticks at 169 ns, 91.0 instead of 97.1 s, 0.76 s (5 \%). Half of the ceiling is a fair expectation for a first design. At 4 MiB the increase is probes, rehashing and the pair table, which a layout can address (the record, its memo words and its table slot are three places; gruszka keeps a value and its memos in one record of 32 bytes); in the giant units the image adds fresh pages for every doubling of a slab (the kernel is 10 \% of their time), which pooled or pre-sized slabs address. Neither removes the miss itself. So 0.3 s on navier-stokes-euler (8 \%), 0.13 s on cslib (2 to 3 \%), 0.4 s on Mathlib (2 to 3 \%).}
\li{\strong{What would have to change.} The session tables of \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~}: \code{\verb~|Machine~} and \code{\verb~|Layout~} (M6), \code{\verb~|Values~} (M1), \code{\verb~|Pairs~} (M4). The designs exist and were not built: a young set in front of the tables for giant sessions, inline arguments of neutral applications, memo words in the record ([rep-machine-M1](rep-machine-M1), [the cost of one machine operation](prob-28KQ)); for the fresh pages, the pooled slabs of [rep-V1](rep-V1), which were measured on the JVM launcher only, where fresh pages cost nothing ([ins-JJ7I](ins-JJ7I): "not measured: the native image with the patch").}
\li{\strong{Risk for the verdict.} The tables are where two values are found to be the same value, so a layout change there is not cost only by nature: a wrong probe is a wrong equality. A change of the cap alone is cost only, and it changes which memo entries exist, so tick counts change with it.}
\li{\strong{Gate.} For a layout change: the full gate of the machine with identical tick counts and record counts, the debug modes that re-compute memo hits and poison dead sessions, the hostile matrix. For a change of cap or of what survives a reset: the gates of the default configuration and the invariance of the verdict under worker count. Then the guard on eight corpora, in the image.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.4 Next in size: the level stores}

\p{Not one of the three on these corpora, but measured and cheap to state. Universe levels are 8.6 s of worker CPU on Mathlib in the image (8.9 \%), 2.9 s on cslib, 1.3 s on navier-stokes-euler, at 2.26 instructions per cycle; and each publication of the pipelined load makes every worker copy the level tables, which costs 1.9 s of worker CPU, 0.9 million page faults and 0.15 s of pauses per Mathlib pass at the default interval (section 4.4): 0.4 s of the pass. One base store shared by the workers, as [ins-258N](ins-258N) proposed and nobody prototyped, would return the 0.4 s (measured, by varying the number of publications), let the file be published in small pieces again, and take a part of the stalled cycles: 38 \% of 8.6 s are stalled, and eight copies of the tables compete for one shared cache. Together 0.5 to 0.6 s on Mathlib (4 \%), 0.15 to 0.2 s on cslib, little on navier-stokes-euler. It touches what is checked only through the store's interface (ids and normal forms must not change); the new hazard is a base that is shared between threads and must be read-only after publication. Modules: \code{\verb~|rlvm-level~}, the level binding of \code{\verb~|rlvm-api~}, \code{\verb~|Levels~} in the machine; gate: the level store's tests, the machine's full gate with identical ticks, the invariance under worker count.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.5 What the three add up to}

\p{On Mathlib in the image: lever 1 by hand 0.8 s, lever 2 directly 0.3 s, lever 3 0.4 s, the level stores 0.5 s: 2.0 s of 14.5 s if all of it came true, to about 12.5 s, with the JVM launcher at 11.3 s and gruszka at 10.8 s on the same quiet host. On navier-stokes-euler 0.2 + 0.05 + 0.3 = 0.55 s of 3.83 s, to about 3.3 s against gruszka's 2.65 s; on cslib 0.3 + 0.1 + 0.13 + 0.15 = 0.7 s of 5.22 s, to about 4.5 s against 4.04 s. The estimates are not additive to the percent, and two of them are guesses with a measured ceiling. What they say together is plain: inside the Community Edition image the levers that this profile can name are worth an eighth to a seventh of the pass, and the rest of the distance to the JVM launcher is the compiler's.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. What the data does not support}

\p{Ideas that come up, with the measurement that speaks against each, on the final default configuration:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Tuning the collector of the image.} 34 collections and 0.27 s of pauses per Mathlib pass, 0.05 s of collector code on the workers, the same pauses as on the JVM: 2 \% of the pass. The collection policy that [log-RCAL](log-RCAL) set took what there was.}
\li{\strong{Flags of the image build} (inliner budgets, \code{\verb~|-march=native~}): within 2 \% on three corpora, section 4.3.}
\li{\strong{More scanner threads, or a parallel load, today}: two scanner threads cost 0.25 s on Mathlib and 0.5 s on navier-stokes-euler and are even on cslib (section 3.1). The pass is bound by its CPU, and splitting the load adds CPU. It becomes right when the workers get faster; it is not a gain by itself.}
\li{\strong{A faster or better laid out hash table for the values.} The probes run at 4.5 to 4.7 instructions per cycle with 7 to 12 \% of their cycles stalled on memory at the default session cap. They are instruction-bound: 25 \% of the workers' CPU is probes because there are 0.5 new records and several probes per tick, not because a probe is slow.}
\li{\strong{A memo in front of universe instantiation} ([ins-258N](ins-258N)): the level item is the first touch of cold data; this profile shows the same (2.26 instructions per cycle, 38 \% of the cycles stalled).}
\li{\strong{Huge pages against TLB misses in the image}: TLB walks are about 1 \% of the workers' cycles. Huge pages would help the page faults, which is another item (section 4.4), and the image has no option for them.}
\li{\strong{The reference engine's fallback}: 60, 16 and 7 units, 1 \% or less of the time inside units (section 3.5). It was 12.5 \% in rep-P10.}
\li{\strong{Numeric computation}: 0.1 to 0.2 \% of the workers.}
\li{\strong{Proof irrelevance}: 0.8 to 1.7 \% inclusive, where it was 27.5 \%.}
\li{\strong{Idle workers, the scheduler, the tail}: parked for 0.8 to 4.2 \% in all, of which the tail is 0.0 to 0.2 \%; the driver's own code is 0.2 \% of the workers' samples.}
\li{\strong{Larger warm sessions} as a way to save steps: they do save steps, 13 to 22 \% on navier-stokes-euler, and each step gets dearer by more (section 5.5). And smaller ones are not uniformly better either: 256 KiB wins 1 to 3 \% on cslib and loses 5 \% on navier-stokes-euler.}
\li{\strong{"gruszka wins by not computing things"}: unfoldings within 3 to 9 \%, ticks within 8 to 22 \%, and the JVM launcher pays gruszka's price per tick on Mathlib and cslib (section 5.2). The exception is four eager-reduction proofs worth 1.3 s of CPU.}
\li{\strong{A list of slow units to repair}: the 20 heaviest units are 3 to 9 \% of the time inside units; the 1,000 heaviest 11 to 27 \%.}
\li{\strong{The benchmark tables' ratios to gruszka as the state of the gap}: they were taken with the other cores of the host busy, which costs gruszka 19 \% and RLVM 6 to 9 \% on Mathlib (section 1.2).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. What was not measured}

\ul{
\li{Anything on x86-64, the arena's architecture; anything with Oracle GraalVM (the figure of the optional profile-guided build is quoted from the benchmark tables).}
\li{Mathlib with one worker; the JVM launcher's hardware events; con-leche, cedar, Init, Std and reachability, which this report does not profile.}
\li{Which allocation sites take the 0.6 million page faults of the workers that are not the level-table copies: a native sample has no caller.}
\li{The recoverable part of the code-quality gap by source changes: the report measures its size per item and that build flags do not touch it.}
\li{What the 8 to 22 \% of extra ticks against gruszka are on cslib and Mathlib, beyond the part that longer sessions give back on navier-stokes-euler: the two tick counters are different instruments, and the excess is spread over every kind and size of declaration.}
\li{Which rule lets gruszka decide the four eager-reduction proofs of \code{\verb~|Batteries.Data.Char.Basic~} in 2,000 ticks.}
\li{gruszka per declaration with 8 workers on cslib and Mathlib (its per-declaration switch distorts such a run); its Mathlib column at the unit level is the one-worker profile of the baseline study of 2026-11-23, taken in other conditions of the host.}
\li{Interleaved rounds for the timings: a cell is one process of several passes unless it says otherwise. Differences below 2 \% are not resolved.}
\li{The image was profiled through a second build with debug information, in the arena container; its passes are within 2 \% of the shipped executable's on the host.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Tools}

\p{The window scripts, the poller, the two classifiers and the table generator are those of [rep-P10](rep-P10), section 7, copied and pointed at the release's distribution; the item map is unchanged. New for this report, all outside the repository: a table of unit numbers and declaration names read from an export (the unit number of the driver is the index of the declaration record in file order, which is also gruszka's index); the join of the driver's unit trace with it and with gruszka's per-declaration lines (smallest time per unit over several passes, totals by kind, size and ratio); the backlog series from the driver's statistics; the method-level comparison of the two platforms; the builds of the symbolised image and of the four variant images from the frozen jars. To re-make the tables after a change: run the windows (JVM launcher with JFR attached after the unprofiled passes; the image unprofiled on the host and under \code{\verb~|perf~} in the arena container; both with \code{\verb~|-X stats-file~} and \code{\verb~|-X unit-trace~}), classify with the item map, and add rules for methods the classifiers report as unassigned.}
}
